  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/cinetodus_froggatti.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Dorsal spines: 1; dorsal soft rays: 7; anal spines: 0; anal soft rays: 17–19.</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern, because it is widespread, common and there are no major threats at present (IUCN 2019).</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/cinetodus_froggatti.htm",
      "html": "<p>Oceania: southern New Guinea and the Roper River system, northern Australia; in coastal waters, estuaries and into fresh water. Type locality: Strickland River, New Guinea.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/cinetodus_froggatti.htm",
      "html": "<p>Inhabits slow-flowing, often turbid rivers and coastal waters. Also occurs in slow-moving freshwater streams, favours turbid coastal shallows and brackish estuaries.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/cinetodus_froggatti.htm",
      "html": "<p>Feeds on gastropod and bivalve mollusks. The food is swallowed whole.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/cinetodus_froggatti.htm",
      "html": "<p>Spawns from October to February in the lower reaches of rivers; males incubate the eggs in their mouth.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/cinetodus_froggatti.htm",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: the specific name froggatti honours Austrian entomologist Walter Wilson Froggatt (1858–1937), \"on whom the whole burden of collecting the zoological specimens obtained during the late expedition of the Bonito [which explored New Guinea] seems to have fallen\".</p>"
    }
  ],
